course: dictionary
lesson: 10963
title: Walrus: a word with a picture
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A walrus is a huge sea animal that lives in the cold Arctic. You can work out what a word means from the words around it: «A walrus has two long tusks, and walruses use their tusks like ice picks to pull their heavy bodies out of the water onto the ice.» Tusks are long, pointed teeth that stick out, and ice picks are sharp tools, so the walrus hooks its tusks into the ice to climb out.
explain: Read the clues carefully. «Walruses find clams on the dark seabed with hundreds of sensitive whiskers and suck them out of their shells.» Clams have shells and live on the seabed, so a walrus eats clams from the bottom of the sea. Sensitive means it can feel very small things, so its whiskers work like fingers in the mud.
explain: Some words have a second, everyday meaning. A «walrus moustache» is a big, thick moustache that droops down, like the whiskers of a walrus. Drooping means hanging down, so you can picture the moustache from the animal, even if you have never seen one.
fact: A walrus is a very large sea mammal of the Arctic, with long white tusks, whiskers, flippers and a thick layer of fat.
fact: Walruses are relatives of seals and sea lions.
fact: A walrus's tusks can be up to about 1 metre long.
fact: Walruses use their whiskers to find clams on the sea floor.
fact: Walruses are in danger as sea ice melts with climate change.
exercise: «Walruses use their tusks, up to about 1 metre long, to haul out onto ice.» How long can a walrus's tusks be? | Only a few centimetres | *Up to about 1 metre | About as long as a bus | Up to about 10 metres
exercise: «Walruses find clams on the dark seabed with hundreds of sensitive whiskers.» What do walruses use their whiskers for? | To keep their tusks clean | To warm their flippers in the sun | To fly through the air | *To find clams on the sea floor
exercise: Find and copy the word that tells you walruses are big: «walruses are huge, need cold seas and eat lots of clams». | clams | *huge | cold | seas
test: In «a herd of walruses», what does herd mean? | A large fish that walruses eat | *A group of walruses | A piece of floating ice | A walrus's home under the sea
test: Jayden reads «a walrus moustache». What does it look like? | *Big, thick and drooping | Small, neat and curly | Shiny and blue | Long and thin like a piece of string
exam: Which animals is the walrus a relative of? | Penguins and gulls | *Seals and sea lions | Elephants and lions | Dolphins and whales
